The Wave-Particle Duality Uncertainty Principle, also known as Heisenberg's uncertainty principle or the indeterminacy principle, is a fundamental concept in quantum mechanics that states there are limits to how precisely we can know certain pairs of complementary variables (such as position and momentum) at the same time. This principle was formulated by Werner Heisenberg and it has significant implications for our understanding of the behavior of particles on an atomic scale, where classical concepts like "particle" or "wave" are insufficient to fully describe quantum objects. 

The Wave-Particle Duality Uncertainty Principle is a fundamental concept in quantum mechanics that states there is a limit to how precisely we can know the position and momentum of particles simultaneously. This principle was formulated by Werner Heisenberg, who introduced it as part of his uncertainty principle. 

